\title{Evaluation Domains, FFTs, and Circle Groups}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Where the polynomials get evaluated, which decides both the prover's asymptotics and which fields are usable at all.
}

\p{
    To work through:
    \ul{
        \li{Smooth multiplicative subgroups and two-adicity; why Goldilocks and BabyBear were chosen for it.}
        \li{Cosets and coset shifts, and why [FRI](dhsorens-0037) folds along them.}
        \li{Mersenne31 has no large smooth subgroup — hence the circle group, and what circle STARKs change about folding and constraint degrees.}
        \li{NTT cost in practice: when the prover is FFT-bound versus hash-bound versus witness-generation-bound.}
        \li{Multilinear systems that avoid FFTs entirely, and what they pay instead.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: Buterin, [Exploring circle STARKs](https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2024/07/23/circlestarks.html); [An introduction to circle STARKs](https://blog.lambdaclass.com/an-introduction-to-circle-starks/).
}
